# Morrow runs on Linux, and a carriage return is not whitespace to any of these tools.
#
# It is worth spelling out, because the failure is not obvious on the machine where the mistake is
# usually made: in `.xinitrc`, a line ending `openbox-session\r` asks for a command called
# "openbox-session\r", so the session does not start; in a `.desktop` file the `Exec=` line ends in a
# CR and the program is never launched; in a shell script a CR lands inside the last variable on the
# line. Git Bash on Windows tolerates CRLF and its grep reads files in text mode, so a Windows
# checkout hides all of it until an ISO boots without a desktop.
* text=auto eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
*.rs text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.xml text eol=lf
*.conf text eol=lf
*.desktop text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
autostart text eol=lf
interfaces text eol=lf
.xinitrc text eol=lf
